lar approach to thinking  about consent and thinking about  recruitment where we need the ability to  um really respond to all those different  nuances as remember that taxonomy that  we looked at before different patient  populations different vulnerabilities  but we could have some standardized tool  to help us navigate those those cases in  similar ways  it's a nice example of an ethical  collaboration between investigators  and  the proposal  calls for  patient participant input so if you read  the kind of paper or you look at some of  the other practices there really is a  focused attention on  more communication with patients  encouraging patients to talk with their  families finding more time to revisit  concerns  and i think that this leads quite nicely  into the last part of this  discussion which is really how do we  move from this framework which was very  much developed from the perspective of  investigators and what they experience  in this space to incorporating the  patient the patient experiences and the  patient values because if we don't quite  understand where patients are when  they're approached to participate or  what their concerns are  what what it's like to kind of  participate in this research  then  you know the framework is not going to  help right  i just want to quickly start by  highlighting a study  done by wexler and her group  published an american i think it was  american journal of bioethics empirical  and it's a study of 22  participants who  consented to join  intraoperative research during their dbs  implantation surgery  and they were consented for the research  and then five days before the surgery um  took place uh they participated in this  study to see how much they could recall  about informed consent right what did  they kind of remember about the research  at that point right so about a week  after they did inform consent but five  days before they were about to do it  um and here's the statistics right so  um  only 22.7  correctly recalled the study risks  um only 36.4 correctly recalled the  study purpose  only 50 percent  correctly recalled the study protocol  and 36.4  correctly recalled the study benefits  um  and i generally like to remain agnostic  about this type of data because it's a  small sample size and there's all sorts  of reasons you know why this might be  the case but at first glance that is  pretty shockingly low right and  not only that but it's like five days  before they're about to go into surgery  and then participate in research  so  part of my lab's work also interviews  patient participants and we have some  qualitative data from participants who  also who also joined intraoperative  research and so this is part of our rf1  dr peradian and myself are co-pis and we  interviewed 15 participants um who were  participating in research  during their surgery for parkinson's  dystonia epilepsy and across three  different types of research studies  and we found some similar things right  so we found very minimal concerns with  risks so here i'm going to share some  quotes from the patients with you i  didn't really think about that and  patients talking about risk if there was  a risk they would have stopped it or  they wouldn't have done something like  that i mean they wouldn't just keep  asking me questions if there were risks  no it didn't occur to me i personally  don't feel that i had any increased risk  in participating in the research  patients also expressed minimal  understanding of the study like the  wechsler paper as well i have no idea i  don't know what they were doing at all  maybe they're mapping the brain maybe  they're just training somebody how to  apply an electrode i have no idea i just  trust that somebody's getting something  out of it  i don't know what the heck i've  contributed to it but i feel good about  it okay  there's not a whole lot of information  out there on it so why not i like being  part of something obviously bigger than  