ping best practices for consent  is the role that trusts played for these  patients  almost in talking about all aspects of  the study and i won't read all of these  quotes but you can look at them a bit as  i'm talking  um  i'll read maybe one or two  so  no i did of course trust him but i  didn't give it a great deal of thought i  just made the decision to participate  if i didn't trust him then i wouldn't  have agreed to have the surgery done at  all so if i'm trusting him with that and  if this is a way that can help anybody  dot dot dot  i think this is quite interesting so you  know what do we kind of make of all of  this um  i think  a controversial take would be something  like this um patients who are approached  to participate in research during  their surgery  think very little about the decision to  join they make it almost immediately  they will say yes likely very quickly  and perhaps with only a vague  understanding of the study goals and  very little concern with the risks  but they'll do so out of feelings of  robust trust and deference to their  doctor and an appreciation for the value  of basic science and helping others  so  what do we do with that how do we  translate that into any practical steps  about best practices  this is the next step that we're at in  my lab and i'm happy to share with you  kind of like our plans of how to mix  together investigators and patients and  you'll see we also have the public in  here  so for us the current steps and next  steps are integrating patient values  with practical proposals so thinking  about really proposing a formal  re-consent process during the surgery  right requiring formal opportunities to  withdraw during the surgery  thinking about having someone other than  the surgeon someone other than the  research team be kind of a part of these  conversations with the patient from the  beginning building that relationship  with a third party not just in the  middle of the surgery but from the  beginning  and thinking about practices that really  build trusting relationships as trust  was such a central feature of of why  participants are joining and what  they're thinking about as they assess  the risks and the benefits how can we  make sure that trust is not exploited  and that the clinical team and the  research team is really invested in that  trusting relationship  we'll move a little bit more quickly  through these so kind of further  exploration of patients  narratives so we're moving to interview  patients in the emu sitting interviewing  patients who have chronic implants  interviewing dci patients  also  further interviews with investigators  and surveys of the public the public has  a really interesting third kind of  perspective on some of these things  they're uninvolved in a way but they're  also a stakeholder how can we think  about what matters to patients by  comparing it to what matters to a kind  of uninvolved party  and then lastly  making progress on practical steps for  involving patients what what kind of  methodology should we be using when we  approach patients to participate in some  of these conversations  i'd like to end by just quickly noting  that um i think to answer all these  questions and i think you know i i hope  that the way we've discussed ethics  today has made you kind of believe this  too there's no way to answer these  questions and what we just talked about  today really without having an  interdisciplinary team and i think a lot  of people talk about you know embedding  ethicist into science as the kind of  standard but um we do both in our lab we  embed ephesus into science we observe  surgeries we go to lab meetings but we  also embed scientists into ethics right  so we we invite scientists to  participate in the discussions to write  the manuscripts and embed them in a  methodology and kind of take  responsibility for those arguments  and i think you know the philosophy of  our lab is really team building trust  and t