s  maintenance making sure that there's  resources in place so that patients who  have this technology  can  make sure it works  can get care um and  you know know about the possibility  that  that technology and those devices may  not be supported forever so especially  if you participate in a study where  the device is new  or the technique is due  um  that device may  you know may not be made forever and so  how can we make sure that patients are  prepared for that and that we don't kind  of leave them with devices in their head  that are not supported or that um you  know nobody kind of will  take care of so a lot of times it  depends on what study you're asking  about if you're if you're asking about  something like a bci study there'll be  very explicit discussions in the consent  process about explanation  um  but you may be asking a different  question which is something like  um  what do we do about um  you know how do we kind of  make sure the devices are developed in  appropriate  ways outside of the patient i think we  would have to ask a tech person that um  but i do think a crucial piece of that  puzzle again is to talk to patients what  are their expectations right after after  those devices are implanted in their  heads do they expect continued care do  they expect to still have access to  their surgeon  what are they thinking they're owed in  virtue of their participation that's a  great question  thank you so much  now what are the main psychosocial  characteristics of the volunteers for  invasive brain research studies  that is also a really good question  so um part of what part of how my  collaboration started with um with a  neurosurgeon was we were asking  questions about altruism we're asking  questions about  um what types of patients  want to volunteer for some of the  studies right so you you know if you  kind of surveyed the public or you had a  larger sample size  you may find that very many people would  not  want to participate in some