her and higher some ephesus and  researchers really feel that you're  you're moving away from compensation and  you're moving towards kind of  incentivizing participation um i don't  think there's a consensus on this um so  that is a a serious area as well  that's a good question  we have one more question a couple  actually we have a couple questions mark  coming in  uh could a team for consent have  downsized such as increased patient  confusion decreased trust  higher cost  these are fantastic questions um  i would say it's possible  i think it depends how you do it right  so  increased um or having a team for  consent just for the purposes of having  a team and having more people and more  conversations i don't think would  would necessarily meet some of those  ethical benchmarks but providing  a really thoughtful  process  i think  would increase trust and decrease  confusion right so sometimes we think  you know providing opportunities for  patients to develop that trust with  multiple members of the team might be  really important  and as would providing a kind of  external person for the patient to  confide in if they had any concerns that  maybe they don't feel comfortable saying  to a clinician  but i do think that's a really good  point that just more people is not  necessarily better  there i think we really need some  focused research um dedicated towards  you know what practices would be would  be optimal that's a great point  thank you so much we have time for two  more questions what do you think  clinician researchers could do  specifically  to improve consent in  um intraoperative research  you know this is a good question too um  i think  as i mentioned in this in this talk  i really do think being quite explicit  during  the surgery about an opportunity to  withdraw i think we tend to think of  um  the intraoperative research as being  kind of a one solid chunk  but it can be 30 minutes long there are  multiple times to check in on the  patient t